\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs30 Demo Introduction\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 This demo shows a conversational database monitoring assistant built with Oracle Private Agent Factory and Select AI. Users ask questions such as \u8220?Are any sessions blocked?\u8221? or \u8220?Which jobs failed in the last 48 hours?\u8221? The Agent queries live monitoring views, explains the findings and asks for clarification when needed. It demonstrates on-demand monitoring without performing database changes.\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs30 Demo Architecture\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa180 {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "file:///home/syazwan/github/paf26.7_livelab/docs/livelab-demo/images/monitoring-agent-architecture.svg"}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf2 Open architecture image}}}\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Solid arrows show workflow, data and model connections. The dashed arrow shows separate repository access. Query results return through the Bridge to the Agent for explanation. The model endpoints may be hosted outside the PAF network.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 For a compact demo, one Autonomous Database can host both the PAF repository and monitoring schemas. Keep their accounts separate. Use another database for the repository if the demo design requires it, and repeat the relevant connection checks against each database.\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs30 Demo Prerequisites\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 The facilitator needs an OCI compartment and permission to provision the demo's database, networking and PAF host or Marketplace stack. Participants need a PAF login with workflow, source and model configuration access. Obtain approved credentials through a secret manager; do not place passwords, private keys, populated connection strings or wallets in project files.\par
