\pard\sa140\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Provide browser-to-PAF HTTPS access, SQLcl-to-database connectivity, PAF-to-database connectivity, PAF-to-Agent-model access and database-to-Select-AI-model access. These are separate paths. For private endpoints, establish private routing and DNS resolution from the actual PAF host/container and the facilitator workstation. Limit inbound access to the demo audience and administration path; use the ports in the actual database connection descriptor.\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs30 End to End Demo Environment Setup\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs25 1. Provision the Autonomous Database\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 In the OCI Console, open {\b Oracle AI Database \u8594? Autonomous AI Database \u8594? Create Autonomous AI Database}. Select the demo compartment, a recognizable display name, Transaction Processing and Oracle AI Database 26ai. Set compute/storage within the demo budget and enter the ADMIN password through the Console. Choose the approved network access option, create the database and wait for {\b Available}. See {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://docs.oracle.com/en-us/iaas/autonomous-database-serverless/doc/autonomous-provision.html"}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf2 Oracle database provisioning}}}.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 From {\b Database connection}, obtain the service descriptor and download a wallet if the selected authentication method requires one. Store it outside the checkout in an approved local secret location. Use its actual host, port and service values when configuring SQLcl and PAF. A public endpoint restricted to approved sources can serve a demo; a private endpoint additionally requires working private connectivity.\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs25 2. Verify SQLcl and the database identity\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Use the SQLcl bundled with Oracle SQL Developer. The path configured in the original lab is:\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 /home/syazwan/.vscode-server/extensions/oracle.sql-developer-26.2.1-linux-x64/dbtools/sqlcl/bin/sql -version\par
\pard\f0\fs22\sa150\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 If that extension version is absent, locate the installed extension's {\f1 dbtools/sqlcl/bin/sql}, verify it with {\f1 -version} and use the verified path. Do not assume another machine has the same extension directory. Start SQLcl with {\f1 /nolog}, then use your approved saved ADMIN connection:\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 connect -name "YOUR_ADMIN_CONNECTION"\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 set echo off\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 set verify off\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 set history off\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 spool off\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 select sys_context('USERENV','SESSION_USER') session_user,\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18        sys_context('USERENV','DB_NAME') db_name,\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18        sys_context('USERENV','CON_NAME') container_name\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 from dual;\par
\pard\f0\fs22\sa150\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Proceed only when the login is ADMIN and the database is the intended demo target. Record its actual {\f1 DB_NAME}; it may differ from the Console display name. Keep credentials in an approved connection store, outside the project.\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs25 3. Prepare the PAF repository accounts\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Check for existing accounts before creating anything:\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 select username, account_status\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 from dba_users\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 where username in ('PAF_REPOSITORY', 'AAI_RO_PAF_REPOSITORY', 'DB_HEALTH_AGENT');\par
\pard\f0\fs22\sa150\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 For a fresh demo, create the repository owner and companion using the template below. Enter password-bearing statements only in the private SQLcl session with history/spooling disabled; replace the placeholders with approved passwords without saving the populated statements. The companion uses the same repository password for this documented setup. Reuse approved accounts if already configured; do not reset an existing deployment's credentials.\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 create user PAF_REPOSITORY identified by "<repository-password>"\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18   default tablespace DATA quota unlimited on DATA;\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 grant create session, create table, create sequence, create trigger,\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18       create type, create procedure, create view, create synonym\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18   to PAF_REPOSITORY;\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 grant read, write on directory DATA_PUMP_DIR to PAF_REPOSITORY;\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 grant select on SYS.V_$PARAMETER to PAF_REPOSITORY;\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 \par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 create user AAI_RO_PAF_REPOSITORY identified by "<repository-password>";\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 grant create session to AAI_RO_PAF_REPOSITORY;\par
\pard\f0\fs22\sa150\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 {\f1 DATA} is the demo's Autonomous tablespace choice; verify it exists first. The owner is dedicated to PAF metadata. These repository grants follow {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/agent-factory/26.7/paias/database-setup.html"}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf2 Oracle database preparation}}}.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Connect as {\f1 PAF_REPOSITORY} and verify:\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 select value from sys.v_$parameter where name = 'max_string_size';\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 select username from all_users where username = 'AAI_RO_PAF_REPOSITORY';\par
\pard\f0\fs22\sa150\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Expect {\f1 EXTENDED} and the companion username. Test a separate companion login too. If the parameter differs, have the DBA resolve the repository prerequisite before installation.\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs25 4. Deploy and initialize Private Agent Factory\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Use {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/agent-factory/26.7/paias/install-oci-marketplace.html"}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf2 Oracle's PAF Marketplace installation}}}:\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 1. Open the PAF Marketplace listing, select {\b Get App \u8594? Launch Stack}, review the terms and choose the demo compartment.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 2. Select a supported shape with available quota, an SSH public key, and the intended VCN/subnet. Oracle lists minimum Flex resources of 8 CPU cores, 16 GB RAM and 120 GB boot volume; verify the current stack requirements.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 3. Create the stack and wait for a successful job. Open {\f1 Agent_Factory_URL} from Outputs over the approved network path. Establish trusted HTTPS for the demo hostname.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 4. In the installation wizard, create the PAF administrator login. Configure the repository connection using {\f1 PAF_REPOSITORY}, its password and descriptor/wallet. Test the connection, install components and configure/test the initial LLM.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 5. Finish installation, sign in and verify the PAF version and home screen.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 The repository connection above is separate from the monitoring data source created next. Restrict host access to the intended demo users; a private-subnet deployment needs a VPN, Bastion or other approved access path. Keep stack outputs and logs containing environment details private.\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs25 5. Create the monitoring account and views\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Reconnect as ADMIN and recheck the database identity from step 2. If {\f1 DB_HEALTH_AGENT} is absent, create it interactively with an independently managed runtime password:\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 create user DB_HEALTH_AGENT identified by "<monitoring-password>";\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 grant create session to DB_HEALTH_AGENT;\par
\pard\f0\fs22\sa150\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 From a SQLcl session started in the repository root, run the supplied first-install script, replacing {\f1 YOUR_DB_NAME} with the verified identifier:\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 @docs/livelab/files/prepare-monitoring.sql YOUR_DB_NAME\par
\pard\f0\fs22\sa150\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 The script checks ADMIN and the expected database, grants cloud-package execution and direct access to five SYS monitoring sources, and creates the five read-only {\f1 MONITOR_*} views. It stops if target objects already exist. Database DDL commits independently; inspect partial state after a failure rather than rerunning blindly. The optional {\f1 DB_HEALTH_OWNER} package schema is not needed for this demo.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Connect through an actual {\f1 DB_HEALTH_AGENT} login and run:\par
\pard\li180\ri180\sa0\f1\fs18 @docs/livelab/files/verify-monitoring.sql YOUR_DB_NAME\par
\pard\f0\fs22\sa150\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Expect five views marked {\f1 VALID} and {\f1 READ_ONLY=Y}, followed by successful bounded reads. No matching sessions or jobs can be a valid result. Keep direct-query results private for comparison with the Agent answers.\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs25 6. Configure model access and the PAF data source\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Have the OCI administrator authorize the model-calling identity for the approved compartment and models. Configure an OCI API signing key for that identity, retaining its private key in the secret manager. Collect the tenancy/user OCIDs, fingerprint, model region and compartment OCID. Use the {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://docs.oracle.com/en-us/iaas/Content/generative-ai/iam-policies.htm"}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf2 OCI Generative AI IAM guidance}}} to scope model access. PAF application login alone does not authorize OCI inference.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 In PAF, open {\b Data Sources \u8594? Add Data Source \u8594? Database}. Create {\b ADB HEALTH MVP}, enter the database descriptor/wallet details and {\f1 DB_HEALTH_AGENT} credential, then test and save. Do not use ADMIN or {\f1 PAF_REPOSITORY} as the monitoring source.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Open {\b LLM Management}, create or reuse {\b llm_model_entry}, configure the approved OCI model and authentication, then test and save. Choose a model that supports the Agent's tool calls. The final Playground query verifies tool use beyond the model connection test.\par
\pard\keepn\sb300\sa150\f0\cf1\b\fs25 7. Create the database Select AI credential and profile\b0\cf0\fs22\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Open {\b Settings \u8594? Select AI Framework} and select {\b ADB HEALTH MVP}. In {\b Credentials}, create or reuse an approved OCI credential with the signing identity details and private key entered only in the credential fields. This database-owned credential is separate from the Agent's LLM registration. See {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/agent-factory/26.7/paias/select-ai.html"}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf2 Oracle Select AI configuration}}}.\par
\pard\sa160\f0\fs22 Create the profile with these values:\par
\trowd\trgaph100\trleft0\clcbpat3\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw5\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw5\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw5\cellx3600\clcbpat3\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw5\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw5\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw5\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw5\cellx10200
